WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.660
Since 2013, we've been treated to two seasons
of Daredevil as well as solo outings for Jessica

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:10.440
Jones, Luke Cage, and Iron Fist — and now
the wait for their team-up series is finally

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:10.940
over.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:15.900
The Defenders season 1 is live on Netflix,
and it's packed with subtle nods and references

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.660
not only to the team members' solo shows and
the wider MCU, but to the original comics,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:21.160
too.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.240
Here are the Easter eggs you might have missed
in The Defenders.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.520
New Harlem Renaissance

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.890
After turning himself over to the law at the
end of his first solo season, Luke Cage is

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.860
fresh out of prison and ready to get back
to protecting Harlem in The Defenders.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.140
When we see him next, Power Man is admiring
the city on the bus, but you probably didn't

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.460
catch what was written on the side of that
bus.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.280
Just for a moment, you see an advertisement
for the New Harlem Renaissance, which is the

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.100
campaign being run by Mariah Dillard, otherwise known as Black Mariah.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.680
She and Shades Alvarez inherited Harlem's
crime syndicate at the end of Luke Cage season

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:54.180
one.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.640
Yellow shirt Luke

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.640
Slowly but surely, we're getting closer to
seeing the Defenders decked out in their traditional

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:01.460
comics garb.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.000
Misty Knight turns up wearing a leather jacket
that kind of resembles her comics costume,

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:11.000
and Danny Rand is starting to wear Iron Fist's
traditional green and yellow colors more regularly.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.240
The most clear-cut fashion influence from
the comics comes in Luke Cage's wardrobe,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:15.740
however.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.960
Before he gets all shot up in a scrap with
Hand henchmen, the bulletproof hero wears

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.440
the plain yellow t-shirt he's rocked on countless
comic book missions.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:24.660
The Incident

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.180
The climactic battle in 2012's Avengers has
been alluded to in various Marvel shows, and

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.560
The Defenders doesn't skip a beat on that
front.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.860
In episode 2, a caller mentions the "Incident"
on Trish Walker's radio show, Trish Talk.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.659
Then, in Karen Page's office, two framed front
pages are still on display that Daredevil

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:46.020
fans will recognize from Ben Urich's time
in that office.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:50.220
One is about "The Battle of New York," while
the one beside it is another Easter egg headline,

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.220
"Harlem Terror," an article about the confrontation
between the Hulk and Abomination that took

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.240
place in 2008's The Incredible Hulk.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.300
Why didn't Page redecorate when she took Ulrich's
office?

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.480
Because they were the best Easter eggs ever,
so why not reuse them?

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:04.460
Lawyer up

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.980
The final scene of the second episode is one
of the biggest nods to the source material

00:02:08.989 --> 00:02:13.510
in the whole season — in fact, it's nearly
identical to a page in Alias #3.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:17.870
Jessica Jones is being questioned about a
murder she didn't commit when, to her surprise,

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.500
Matt Murdock turns up and announces himself
as her attorney.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:22.000
Stop talking"

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:22.620
"What?"

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:23.500
"This is over."

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:25.720
"Who the Hell are you?"

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.380
"My name is Matthew Murdock.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.080
I'm your attorney."

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.160
There are a few differences, but this is still
an unmistakable nod to Alias.

00:02:33.169 --> 00:02:37.790
The five-part series was the main inspiration
for Jessica Jones season 1, which was a good

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.069
move according to star Krysten Ritter.

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:43.689
She told Time: "Actually Alias was my first
time reading a comic book.

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:44.720
And I loved them."

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.180
Stan the man

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:52.180
The obligatory Stan Lee cameo comes 26 minutes
into episode 3, when the Marvel mainstay can

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.180
be seen in a few consecutive shots.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.880
As Jessica Jones follows Matt Murdock down
a busy New York street, they pass by a kiosk

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.700
adorned with Lee's grinning face.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:02.540
Theme song cameo

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:07.620
During a scene in episode 6, Murdock sits
down at a piano and starts playing a song,

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.720
stopping only when he realizes one of the
keys sounds dull.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.120
Jessica pops the lid and finds a set of blueprints
for the Midland Circle building.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.320
It's an interesting plot development, but
equally as interesting is the tune Murdock

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.400
was playing: The Defenders theme song.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.400
White privilege

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.180
Iron Fist has the unfortunate distinction
of being the only MCU Netflix show to get

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:39.600
slammed hard by critics.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.000
Some of that criticism was aimed at the fight
scenes and at leading man Finn Jones himself.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.280
But what really ruined Iron Fist in the eyes
of its detractors was its worn-out story of

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.800
a privileged young white dude.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.299
Even The Defenders took a shot at him for
it.

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:56.870
In episode 3, Luke Cage has a sit-down with
Danny Rand and tells him exactly how it is.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.060
"The difference is I live on their block.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.400
The difference is I'm not some billionaire
white boy that takes justice into his own

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.620
hands and slams a black kid against a wall
because of his personal vendetta.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.959
Hopefully that speech will spark some character
development for Rand, but it pretty much sums

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.860
up the first season of Iron Fist for a lot
of fans.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.100
Misty's arm

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.100
This season of The Defenders didn't end particularly
well for Misty Knight.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.940
Right when Bakuto is about to
take off Claire Temple's head, Knight pushes

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.420
her out of harm's way — losing her arm in
the process.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:29.440
For those unfamiliar with Misty Knight's story
in the comics, it must have seemed like a

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.880
pretty horrific way for the character to finish
the series.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.060
But those in the know were already thinking
about what was to come.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.160
In the comics, Knight loses her arm during
a terrorist attack, but she's fitted with

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.620
a new bionic one by none other than Tony Stark.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.080
The Night Nurse

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.400
The seeds of the budding friendship between
Misty Knight and Colleen Wing are shown at

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.580
the end of The Defenders, with Colleen waiting
by Knight's hospital bed for her to wake up.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.260
There's also a subtle comic book Easter egg
in this scene.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:58.740
When Wing stands up, a white board with patient
information can be seen over her shoulder,

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.880
and on it is the name of the nurse on duty
— L. Carter.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.340
This is a nod to nurse Linda Carter, a.k.a.
the Night Nurse, who often helped Daredevil

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:07.040
in the comics.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:11.780
Her role of superhero caregiver has been passed
on to Claire Temple in the show, but it's

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.360
still a pretty cool reference.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:14.660
Sister Maggie

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.560
After the Defenders blow up Midland Circle,
Matt Murdock wakes up in a strange bed, but

00:05:19.571 --> 00:05:23.810
he isn't in a hospital - he's being cared
for in a nunnery of some kind, at which point

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:24.800
a nun says this:

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:26.780
"Get Maggie."

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.820
If you're familiar with Daredevil's backstory,
you may recognize the comics reference.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.440
The show never told us much about Matt Murdock's
mother.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.840
"What about the mother?

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:36.580
She dead?"

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:37.840
"Ah no.. she's..."

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:40.340
"Well, that's another story."

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.950
But comics readers know she left the family
after falling mentally ill and wound up being

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:47.880
taken in by the church, where she took on
the name Margaret.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.780
Did The Defenders just tease a family reunion
for Daredevil season 3?

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:52.940
Thanks for watching!

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.580
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.240
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

